  - [UpToDate: Treatment of recurrent and metastatic nasopharyngeal carcinoma](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/treatment-of-recurrent-and-metastatic-nasopharyngeal-carcinoma?search=nasopharyngeal+carcinoma&topicRef=3372&source=see_link)

## Epidemiology, etiology and diagnosis of nasopharyngeal carcinoma

- **Definition** - predimonant tumour type of the nasopharynx, differing from other SCCs in epidemiology, histology, natural history, and response to treatment
- **Epidemiology** - geographically variable:
  - <u>Incidence</u> - 25/100,000/y in HK and southern China (cf 0.5-2/100,000/y in Western countries)
  - <u>Demographic</u>:
    - **Age** - peak incidence in 50-59y (minor incidence peak in adolescence and young adults)
    - **Sex** - male preponderance (M:F = 2-3:1)
- **Etiology and risk factors of NPC:**
  - **Epstein-Barr virus** (EBV) - as primary etiologic agent in the pathogenesis of NPC w/ detection of both EBV DNA and gene expression in precursor lesions and tumour cells
  - **Diet** - diet rich in <u>nitrosamines</u> (e.g. salted fish, feermented foods) are thought to increase risk of NPC
  - **Genetic factors** - evidence of familial clusters w/ increased risk of NPC if first-degree relatives have NPC
- **Histological classification of NPC** - WHO classification:
  - **Keratinizing SCC** - a/w keratinization and is the most common histology seen in sporadic (non-EBV associated) NPC
  - **Non-keratinizing SCC** - non-keratinizing subtype is further subdivied by differentiation:
    - <u>Undifferentiated, nonkeratinizing SCC</u> - most common histology seen in endemic forms of NPC, w/ strong associations w/ Epstein-Barr virus and has a more favorable prognosis
    - <u>Differentiated nonkeratinizing SCC</u> - not the commonest histology seen in NPC
  - **Basaloid SCC** - w/ few reported cases and are notable for aggresive clinical course and poor prognosis
- **Clinical features of NPC** - classical triad of 1) neck mass, 2) nasal obstruction w/ epistaxis, and 3) severe OME is infrequent but extremely suggestive of NPC:
  - **Asymptomatic** - many patients remain asymptomatic for a prolonged period especially for NPC arising from the fossa of Rosenmuller which is clinically occult
  - **Features of local tumour:**
    - <u>Nasal obstruction</u> - typically unilateral, but ocassionally bilateral nasal obstruction if tumour is large
    - <u>Epistaxis</u> - typically mild and usually noticed first thing in the morning
  - **Features of local invasion:**
    - <u>Otitis media w/ effusion</u> - presentation w/ unilateral hearing loss, otalgia w/ evidence of effusion on otoscope as a result of obstruction of the Eustachian tube
    - <u>Tinnitus</u> - due to obstruction resulting in eustachian tube dysfunction
    - <u>Diplopia</u> - due to paracavernous sinus tumour invasion resulting in palsies of CN III, IV or VI
    - <u>Hemifacial numbness</u> - due to paracavernous sinus tumour invasion resulting in CN V palsy
    - <u>Headache</u> - due to intracranial spread via th skull base
  - **Features of nodal metastasis** (75-90% at presentation) - presentation as <u>neck mass</u> (unilateral or bilateral), which are LN metastasis to level 2 and 5 LNs
  - **Features of distant metastasis** (5-11% at presentation) - most common to the bone (75% of distant metastasis), lung, liver, or distant LNs
  - **Paraneopastic phenomenon:**
    - Neutrophilia
    - Pyrexia of unknown origin
    - Hypertrophic osteoarthropathy
    - Dermatomyositis or polymyositis
- **Signs of NPC** - examination of the neck, otoscope, cranial nerves and nasoendoscopy:
  - <u>Otoscope</u> - look for OME bilaterally
  - <u>Cranial nerve examination</u> - assessment of CN V and EOM:
    - Hemifacial numbness if CN V affected
    - Diplopia if CN III, IV or VI affected
  - <u>Neck examination</u> - palpation of of cervical LN (note for LN in level II or V)
  - <u>Nasoendoscopy</u> - visualisation of tumour and endoscopic-guided Bx of the primary tumour for histological Dx
- **Ix:**
  - **Routine bloods** - CBC, LRFT, EBV DNA level
    - <u>LFT</u> - ALP for bone metastasis
    - <u>EBV DNA level</u>:
      - Pretreatment levels are prognostic for survial outcomes
      - High EBV DNA load \>= 4000 copies/mL are suggestive of distant metastasis (warranting PET-CT)
  - **Imaging** - CT/ MRI of the skull base, nasopharynx and neck, +/- CXR or PET-CT
- **Staging system of NPC** - AJCC 9th edition TNM staging:
  - **Principles of staging of NPC:**
    - <u>T-staging</u> - based on involvement and invasion of adjacent anatomical structures
    - <u>N-staging</u> - based on 1) laterality, 2) size, 3) level, and 4) presence of extranodal invasion
    - <u>M-staging</u> - based on presence and number of metastatic lesions
  - **TNM staging of NPC:** ![](~/Documents/notes/img/ajcc_9e_tnm_staging_npc_t_stage.png) ![](~/Documents/notes/img/ajcc_93_tnm_staging_npc_n_stage.png) 
  ![](~/Documents/notes/img/ajcc_9e_tnm_staging_npc_m_stage.png)
  - **Prognostic staging of NPC:**
    - <u>Stage I or II</u> - \<= T3N2 disease
    - <u>Stage III</u> - T4N0 or N3 disease
    - <u>Stage IV</u> - M1 disease

    
    ![](~/Documents/notes/img/prognostic_stage_group_of_npc.png)

## Treatment of early and locoregionally advanced nasopharyngeal carcinoma

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Early stage NPC</u> - Stage IA disease, i.e. patients w/ T1-2N0M0 disease
  - <u>Intermediate stage NPC</u> - Stage IB and II disease, i.e. patients w/ T0-2 primary tumour, N0-2, and MO disease
  - <u>Locoregionally advanced stage NPC</u> - Stage III disease, i.e. T4 or N3 disease with no distant metastasis
  - <u>Metastatic stage NPC</u> - M1 disease
- **Principles of RT in NPC** - raditionally treated w/ RT alone as anatomical locations limit surgical approaches, but NPC is a radiosensitive tumour:
  - **Radiosensitivity of NPC** - generally a radiosensitive tumour:
    - Undifferentiated non-keratinising NPC (endemic forms) appears to be particularly radiosensitive and may be associated with higher OS than nonendemic disease
    - However, insufficient evidence to suggest that treatment should differ based on histopathologic classification
  - **RT techniques** - contemporary external beam RT techniques become standard of care:
    - IMRT, VMAT +/- proton therapy demonstrates better disease control w/ less toxicities and fewer serious complications than 2DRT (Phase III RCT demonstrates better 5y OS and decreased toxicity and complication)
    - Adequate patient immobilisation and verification of daily set up accuracy important by image guided RT
    - Adapative replanning in cases where there is significant change in patient anatomy, rapid tumour shrinkage, patient swelling or weight loss
  - **Dosing and schedule:**
    - 66-70 Gy for macroscopic disease w/ conventional fractionation
    - Accelerated fractionation may be as an alternative, but does not appear to improve survival and increase toxicity
  - **Coverage:**
    - Node +ve disease - cover primary tumour and bilateral neck nodal basements from retropharyngeal LN to lateral neck levels II-IV and V
    - Some study demonstrated that reduced coverage may yield similar outcomes in N1 or N0 disease (e.g. omission of contralateral radiation for N1 disease, or bilaterally for N0 disease)
- **Role of surgery:**
  - Limited role in early or intermediate stage NPC (potentially role of neck disection in residual nodal disease after RT)
  - Role in recurrent or locally-advanced NPC - e.g. isolated neck recurrence or small localised recurrence
- **Stage-specific Mx of early, intermediate or locoregionally advanced NPC:**
  - <u>Early stage NPC</u> - RT alone
  - <u>Intermediate stage NPC</u> - based on risk stratification by nodal size, extranodal invasion, and EBV DNA load:
    - High-risk intermediate stage NPC - concurrent chemoirradiation
    - Low-risk intermediate stage NPC - RT alone
  - <u>Locoregionally advanced NPC</u> - induction chemotherapy followed by concurrent chemoirradiation (concurrent chemoirradiation w/ adjuvant chemothrapy as an alternative)
- **Induction chemotherapy:**
  - <u>Indications of induction chemotherapy</u> - locoregionally advanced NPC:
    - In a meta-analysis compariing induction chemotherapy w/ concurrent chemoradiation to oncurrent chemoradiation alone (MAP-NPC), addition of induction chemotherapy to chemoradiation cofers an OS benefit (HR 0.75 \[95% CI 0.59-0,96\] in induction chemotherapy w/ taxanes, vs HR 0.81 \[95% CI 0.69-0.95\] in induction chemotherapy w/o taxanes)
    - RCTs included also demonstrate:
      - Improved failure-free survival
      - Improved distant metastasis-free survival
      - Non-statistically significant improvement in locoregional control
  - <u>Regimen</u>:
    - Gemcitabine plus cisplatin (GP)
    - Cisplatin plus docetaxel and fluorouracil (TPF)
    - Cisplatin plus paclitaxel and capecitabine (TPC)
- **Concurrent chemoradiation** - concurrent chemoradiation over RT due to 1) chemosensitizing properties, and 2) modest effect of chemosensitizing agent on distant micrometastasis:
  - <u>Indications</u>:
    - High risk intermediate stage NPC (i.e. cervical node \> 3cm, extranodal extension, or EBV DNA \>= 4000 copies/ml)
    - Locoregionally advanced NPC
  - <u>Evidence for concurrent chemoradiation</u>:
    - United State Intergroup 0099 trial demonstrate concurrent chemoradiation + adjuvant chemotherapy confers OS (3y OS 78% vs 47%) and PFS (3y PFS 69% vs 24%) benefits
    - MAC-NPC includes 7 RCTs comparing concurrent chemoradiation alone w/ RT alone, demonstrating improvement in both OS and PFS
    - Limited evidence for RT alone in selected patients after induction therapy
  - <u>Selection of chemosensitizing agent</u>:
    - Cisplatin (40 mg/m2 weekly)
    - Carboplatin (for patients w/ borderline renal function, pre-existing hearing loss, tinnitus, olde age or ECOG \>= 2)
- **Prognosis** - stage dependent (note data using 7th edition staging): 
![](../img/stage_dependent_prognosis_of_npc.png)

## Treatment of recurrent and metastatic nasopharyngeal carcinoma

- **Tx of metastatic NPC** - systemic therapy including systemic chemotherapy and IO +/- consolidation w/ radiotherapy
- **Tx of recurrent NPC** - dependent on staging:
  - <u>Small local recurrence or isolated nodal recurrence</u> - salvage surgery possible
  - <u>Irresectable recurrence</u> - reirrdiation
  - <u>Distant recurrence present</u> - Tx as metastatic NPC
- **Chemoimmunotherapy** - gemcitabine + cisplatin + toripalimab as first line for Tx-naive patient
- **Consolidation w/ RT** - indicated for patients w/ de novo oligometastatic NPC w/ good PS and at least partial response to systemic chemo

# Splenomegaly, Splenic Disorders, Splenectomy - Literature Search on UpToDate <span class="tag" tag-name="Reference"><span class="smallcaps">Reference</span></span>

- **Reference:**
  - [UpToDate: Splenomegaly and other splenic disorders in adults](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/splenomegaly-and-other-splenic-disorders-in-adults?search=splenomegaly&source=search_result&selectedTitle=1~150&usage_type=default&display_rank=1#H23)
  - [UpToDate: Elective (diagnostic or therapeutic) splenectomy](https://www.uptodate.com.eproxy.lib.hku.hk/contents/elective-diagnostic-or-therapeutic-splenectomy?search=splenomegaly&topicRef=7134&source=see_link)

## Splenomegaly and other splenic disorders in adult

- **Definition and terminology:**
  - <u>Splenomegaly</u> - increased spleen size
  - <u>Hypersplenism</u> - splenic sequestration and/or destruction of blood cells extensive enough to result in one or more cytopenias
- **DDx of splenomegaly** - splenomegaly is caused by:
  - **Vascular congestion** - increased pressure resulting in accumulation of blood in the spleen:
    - <u>Portal HTN</u> - pre-hepatic, intra-hepatic, and post-hepatic
    - <u>Vascular occlusion</u> - Budd Chiari syndrome, portal vein thrombosis, splenic vein thrombosis
  - **Increased splenic activity:**
    - <u>Increased red pulp activity</u> - red cell disorders, particularly haemolytic anaemia
    - <u>Increased white pulp activity</u>:
      - Autoimmune cytopenias (e.g. AIHA, ATP, autoimmune neutropenia, Felty syndrome, SLE)
      - Infection - e.g. endocarditis, TB, EBV, CMV, malaria, histoplasmosis
  - **Infiltration:**
    - <u>Haematological infiltration</u> - leukaemia, lymphoma, myeloproliferative neoplasms (e.g. CML, PV, ET), extramedullary haematopoiesis (e.g. Myelofibrosis, Thalasssaemia major)
    - <u>Non-haematological infiltration</u> - lysosomal storage diseases (e.g. Gaucher's disease), sarcoidosis
- **Clinical features of splenomegaly:**
  - **Features of splenomegaly:**
    - <u>Abdominal bloating and early satiety</u> - due to gastric compression
    - <u>Abdominal pain</u> - non-specific discomfort refered to the chest or left shoulder
  - **Features related to underlying cause:**
    - <u>Pharyngitis</u> - suggestive of infectious mononucleosis
    - <u>Constitutional B symptoms</u> - suggestive of lymphoma
    - <u>Complication of cirrhosis</u> - e.g. ascites, variceal bleeding, hepatic encephalopathy
- **P/E** - temperature, general examination, abdominal examination, and palpation of regional LNs:
  - **Fever** - may be seen w/ an infection, but <u>PUO</u> may reflect underlying haematological malignancy
  - **General examination:**
    - <u>Pallor</u> - reflects underlying anaemia which may be a/w the splenomegaly
    - <u>Ruddy complexion</u> - may be seen in PV
    - <u>Stigmata of chronic liver disease</u> - suggestive of underlying liver disease
    - <u>Ascites or peripheral oedema</u> - reflects underlying portal hypertension and vascular occlusion
  - **Abdominal examination:**
    - <u>Hepatosplenomegaly</u> - reflects underlying liver disease, lymphoproliferative, myeloproliferative disease or infiltrative disease
    - <u>Splenic tenderness</u> - suggestive of underlying infarction, rupture or acute infection
  - **Examination of LN** - lymphadenopathy reflects underlying lymphoproliferative disease
- **Ix:**
  - **CBC** - cytopenias may be a/w 1) hypersplenism, 2) autoimmune cytopenias, 3) herediatary haemolytic anaemia, or 4) bone marrow infiltration
  - **Peripheral blood smear** - certain abnormalities may prompt BM examination
    - <u>Immature or abnormal WBCs</u> - suggestive of underlying lymphoproliferative or myeloproliferative neoplasms
    - <u>Spherocytosis</u> - suggestive of underlying hereditary spherocytosis or AIHA
    - <u>Tear drop cells</u> - myelofibrosis or thalassaemia
  - **LFT** - to assess contribution of liver disease, although elevation of transaminases is non-specific and may reflect underlying infection
  - **BM examiantion** - indicated when:
    - 1\. Splenomegaly w/ leukocytosis and abnormal white cells on PBS
    - 2\. Splenomeegaly with thrombocytosis or erythrocytosis
  - **LN biopsy** - if splenomegaly w/ peripheral lymphadenopathy suggestive of lymphoma
  - **Additional Ix:**
    - <u>Septic workup</u> - if infection is suspected
    - <u>HIV testing</u> - may be appropriate if no other causes of splenomegaly is immediately apparent
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx:**
    - <u>Dx and Tx of underlying cause</u> - Tx of underlying condition may reduce splenic size and lead to symptomatic improvement
    - <u>Sport participation and fall risk</u> - typically for individuals w/ infectious mononucleosis, which carries small but increased risk of splenic rupture and is potentially life threatening
    - <u>Splenectomy</u> - may be indicated for unexplained splenectomy or other autoimmune cytopenias
